from .common import *
from .lg import S


def theory(B):
    B.h2("12.8  Electronic structure of benzyne in depth")
    B.ul(["<b>Orbital picture.</b> Benzyne (1,2-didehydrobenzene, C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>) retains the aromatic sextet in the π system perpendicular to the ring (six p orbitals, one on each carbon). The 'triple bond' is a formal description: the two ortho carbons each have an sp<sup>2</sup> orbital that would have held a C–H or C–X bond; these two orbitals point outward in the ring plane and lie nearly parallel, overlapping only sideways and poorly (the ring geometry forces an inter-orbital angle of 120° rather than 180°). The resulting <b>in-plane 'π' bond is weak</b> and high in energy, and the corresponding antibonding orbital is low-lying, which makes benzyne a strong electrophile (LUMO energy ≈ −1.0 eV) and a very reactive dienophile.",
          "<b>Geometry and spectroscopy.</b> Computed C≡C ≈ 1.24 Å (between a double bond, 1.34, and a triple bond, 1.20), with the ring angles at the 'triple bond' ≈ 127° and a distorted hexagon. Matrix-isolation IR (Chapman, 1973, from phthaloyl peroxide or benzocyclobutenedione photolysis at 8 K) shows a band near 1846 cm<sup>−1</sup> assigned to the strained C≡C stretch, lower than that of a normal alkyne (≈ 2150) because of the weak bond. The ground state is a closed-shell <b>singlet</b>; its first triplet is ≈ 37 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup> above.",
          "<b>Resonance forms.</b> Benzyne is described by the hybrid of the cumulene-like form (four electrons in two in-plane orbitals forming a weak bond) and a zwitterionic/diradical form (C<sup>+</sup>/C<sup>−</sup>), which explains both its electrophilicity at carbon and its ability to react with nucleophiles in two orientations. The in-plane bond does <i>not</i> interact with the aromatic π system (orthogonal), so benzyne retains its aromatic stabilisation.",
          "<b>Why alkynes are linear but benzyne is not.</b> A linear sp-hybridised carbon needs 180°; the six-membered ring cannot accommodate it. The cost is ≈ 50 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup> of strain, the energy that is released when benzyne reacts; cyclohexyne, cycloheptyne and cyclooctyne are analogous strained cycloalkynes (cyclooctyne is the smallest stable one; strain-promoted azide–alkyne click chemistry uses it).",
          "<b>The three isomers.</b> 1,2-Didehydrobenzene (ortho-benzyne, the usual one), 1,3-didehydrobenzene (meta-benzyne) and 1,4-didehydrobenzene (para-benzyne) are all C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>. Meta- and para-benzyne are singlet diradicals with the two radical centres separated by one or two carbons; <b>p-benzyne</b> is formed by the <b>Bergman cyclisation</b> of (Z)-hex-3-ene-1,5-diynes at 200 °C, and by the 'enediyne' antitumour antibiotics (calicheamicin, esperamicin, dynemicin, neocarzinostatin) under physiological conditions: the generated p-benzyne abstracts two H atoms from DNA sugar rings (C-4′, C-5′), leading to strand cleavage and cell death. Enyne-allenes cyclise by the analogous Myers–Saito reaction (to α,3-didehydrotoluene)."])
    B.h2("12.9  Generation of benzyne: complete catalogue")
    B.table(["Precursor", "Conditions", "Mechanism / remarks"],
        [["Aryl halide (ArX with ortho H)", "NaNH<sub>2</sub>/NH<sub>3</sub>(l), −33 °C; KNH<sub>2</sub>; LDA; LiTMP; PhLi; KOtBu/DMSO", "E2 or E1cB: base removes the ortho H (acidified by the inductive effect of X), X<sup>−</sup> leaves. Elimination rate (leaving group in the E1cB step) Br &gt; I &gt; Cl ≫ F (in NH<sub>3</sub>/KNH<sub>2</sub>); however, for F the ortho H is the most acidic (strong −I) and ArF reacts with LiTMP/PhLi via ArLi, in which F<sup>−</sup> is lost slowly: the C–F bond is strong. Substrates with no ortho H (2,6-disubstituted halides) do not give benzyne"],
         ["o-Dihaloarenes + Mg or Li", "o-BrC<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>F + Mg, THF; o-FC<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>Li (−78 °C → warm)", "Wittig 1942: ArMgX/ArLi with ortho-F eliminates MgXF/LiF at room temperature; in the presence of furan gives the 1,4-epoxy-1,4-dihydronaphthalene adduct, the first trapping evidence"],
         ["Anthranilic acid (via diazonium)", "o-H<sub>2</sub>NC<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>COOH + isoamyl nitrite (or HONO) → benzenediazonium-2-carboxylate (zwitterion), Δ", "loses CO<sub>2</sub> and N<sub>2</sub> (Stiles–Miller 1960); aprotic route; the zwitterion is explosive when dry, so is prepared in situ"],
         ["1-Aminobenzotriazole", "Pb(OAc)<sub>4</sub> or NBS", "Campbell–Rees: oxidation gives benzyne + 2 N<sub>2</sub>"],
         ["Phthaloyl peroxide, benzothiadiazole dioxide, benzocyclobutenedione", "hν or Δ", "photolytic routes used for matrix isolation and spectroscopy"],
         ["2-(Trimethylsilyl)aryl triflates (Kobayashi, 1983)", "CsF or TBAF, MeCN or THF, 25 °C", "fluoride attacks Si; C–Si electrons form the in-plane bond while triflate leaves: the standard mild method (no strong base); used for natural-product synthesis"],
         ["o-(Trimethylsilyl)aryl iodonium triflates", "Bu<sub>4</sub>N<sup>+</sup>F<sup>−</sup>", "Kitamura: loses PhI as leaving group"],
         ["Hexadehydro-Diels–Alder (HDDA)", "triyne (1,3-diyne + diynophile), 80–120 °C", "Hoye: thermal [4+2] of a diyne with an alkyne gives a benzyne from acyclic precursors without any base or halide (Hoye 2012)"],
         ["Benzene-1,2-diazonium and aryl diazonium salts + base", "ArN<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>, ortho-H, base", "rare"],
         ["Photolysis of o-iodophenyl... or flash vacuum pyrolysis", "FVP of phthalic anhydride (950 °C)", "gas-phase benzyne; mass spectrometric detection"]],
        widths=[26, 32, 42])
    B.h2("12.10  Evidence for benzyne as an intermediate: Roberts, trapping, isotope effects")
    B.ul(["<b>Roberts’ <sup>14</sup>C-labelling experiment (1953).</b> Chlorobenzene labelled with <sup>14</sup>C at C-1 reacts with KNH<sub>2</sub> in liquid NH<sub>3</sub> to give aniline with 50 % <sup>14</sup>C at C-1 and 50 % at C-2. A direct substitution (S<sub>N</sub>Ar, ipso) would have given 100 % C-1 aniline. The 1 : 1 distribution requires a symmetrical intermediate in which C-1 and C-2 are equivalent: benzyne.",
          "<b>Absence of ortho hydrogen.</b> 2-Bromo-1,3-dimethylbenzene (both ortho positions blocked by CH<sub>3</sub>) does not react with KNH<sub>2</sub>; 2,6-dimethylhalobenzenes are inert — in contrast, S<sub>N</sub>Ar reactions are not blocked by methyl groups in the 2,6-positions.",
          "<b>Isotope effect and exchange.</b> Fluorobenzene-2-d and bromobenzene-2-d: H/D exchange at the ortho position occurs faster than substitution in NH<sub>3</sub>/KNH<sub>2</sub> for the weakly leaving fluorine, showing a reversible aryl carbanion (E1cB), while for bromobenzene the removal of H is rate-limiting (E2-like, k<sub>H</sub>/k<sub>D</sub> ≈ 5.5).",
          "<b>Trapping.</b> Benzyne gives Diels–Alder adducts with furan (1,4-epoxy-1,4-dihydronaphthalene), anthracene (triptycene, Wittig 1956), tetraphenylcyclopentadienone (1,2,3,4-tetraphenylnaphthalene after loss of CO), cyclopentadiene (benzonorbornadiene), 2,5-dimethylfuran and 1,3-diphenylisobenzofuran, even when no nucleophile is present. Dimer biphenylene and trimer triphenylene arise in the absence of traps.",
          "<b>Cine substitution.</b> Substituted halobenzenes give mixtures of isomers in which the nucleophile enters at the carbon bearing the leaving group (<i>ipso</i>) or the adjacent carbon (<i>cine</i>): o-bromoanisole → m-anisidine only; p-chlorotoluene → p- and m-toluidine (1 : 1); m-chlorotoluene → o-, m-, p-toluidine; o-chlorotoluene → o- and m-toluidine (≈ 1 : 1). The incoming group is never found at positions ‘para’ to the leaving group unless the substituent pattern allows it.",
          "<b>Spectroscopic detection.</b> Matrix IR (1846 cm<sup>−1</sup>), NMR of benzyne trapped inside a hemicarcerand (Cram, 1997: the cage prevents dimerisation; <sup>13</sup>C shift of the triple-bond carbons 182 ppm), and gas-phase photoionisation mass spectrometry."])
    B.h2("12.11  Regiochemistry of nucleophilic addition to substituted benzynes: Roberts and Gilchrist rules")
    B.p("For a benzyne with a substituent Z at C-3 (adjacent to the triple bond) or C-4 (one carbon removed), the nucleophile attacks the position that gives the more stable aryl carbanion. The anion is in the plane of the ring and cannot be stabilised by resonance; its stability is controlled by <b>inductive/field effects</b> (and in some cases by coordination of the metal cation). Rules:")
    B.table(["Substituent position", "Z", "Preferred site of Nu<sup>−</sup> attack", "Product"],
        [["3-Z-benzyne (Z adjacent)", "−I (OMe, OR, F, Cl, CF<sub>3</sub>, NR<sub>3</sub><sup>+</sup>)", "C-1 (remote from Z), so the carbanion at C-2 is adjacent to Z and is stabilised inductively", "meta-substituted product predominates (e.g. o-bromoanisole → m-anisidine, &gt; 99 %)"],
         ["3-Z-benzyne", "+I (CH<sub>3</sub>)", "~1 : 1 (small steric preference for C-1)", "mixture of o- and m-"],
         ["3-Z-benzyne", "large steric group (t-Bu, SiMe<sub>3</sub>)", "C-1 (steric)", "meta"],
         ["4-Z-benzyne (Z one carbon removed)", "−I (OMe, CF<sub>3</sub>)", "attack at C-1 gives carbanion at C-2 (meta to Z); attack at C-2 gives carbanion at C-1 (para to Z). The carbanion nearer to Z is favoured, but the effect is small (the distances are similar)", "mixture of meta and para (m ≥ p; e.g. p-chloroanisole → ca. 1 : 1)"],
         ["4-Z-benzyne", "alkyl", "~1 : 1", "m : p ≈ 1 : 1"],
         ["Distortion model (Garg–Houk, 2010)", "—", "aryne is distorted by the substituent so that the more linear carbon (bigger bond angle, more electrophilic) is attacked: 3-OMe: C-1 attack; indolynes and 3,4-pyridynes with the substituent-induced distortion", "predicts selectivity in heteroarynes: 3,4-pyridyne with 5-bromo-substituent → attack at C-3 selectively"]],
        widths=[22, 24, 34, 20])
    B.h2("12.12  Reactions of benzyne: a systematic classification")
    B.table(["Class", "Partner", "Product", "Notes"],
        [["Nucleophilic addition–protonation", "NH<sub>2</sub><sup>−</sup>, RO<sup>−</sup>, RS<sup>−</sup>, R<sub>2</sub>N<sup>−</sup>, CN<sup>−</sup>, enolates, ArLi, RMgX, amines (neutral), halide, azide", "ArNu after protonation of the aryl anion by the solvent (NH<sub>3</sub>, ROH)", "α-arylation of ketones/nitriles (Caubère: NaNH<sub>2</sub>/ t-BuONa 'complex base' with ArX and a ketone); intramolecular attack gives rings (indoles, benzofurans)"],
         ["Diels–Alder [4+2]", "furan, cyclopentadiene, anthracene, pyrrole (N-protected), isobenzofuran, cyclohexadiene, α-pyrone, tetracyclone, 2-pyridones", "benzo-fused bicyclics, triptycene, naphthalenes (with extrusion of CO<sub>2</sub> or CO)", "most reliable trapping; the endo/exo and π-facial selectivity are governed by sterics"],
         ["[2+2] cycloaddition", "enol ethers, ketene acetals, alkenes with e-donors, ketenes", "benzocyclobutenes (with ArH loses nothing)", "Ar + CH<sub>2</sub>=C(OR)<sub>2</sub> → 1,1-dialkoxybenzocyclobutene; in the absence of trap, 2 ArH → biphenylene"],
         ["[3+2] 1,3-dipolar", "azides, nitrones, diazo compounds, nitrile oxides, sydnones", "benzotriazoles, benzisoxazolines, indazoles", "click-type; mild with Kobayashi precursors"],
         ["Ene reaction", "propene, allylbenzene, alkenes with allylic H", "allylbenzenes (Ar–C–C=C with H transfer)", "benzyne acts as enophile: a concerted six-membered TS; e.g. benzyne + propene → allylbenzene"],
         ["σ-Bond insertion", "C–C (β-ketoester α-acylation), C–N (amides), N–Si, S–S, Sn–Sn, C–Cl", "ortho-disubstituted arenes", "Stoltz acyl-alkylation of β-ketoesters (aryne inserts into the C–C bond of the enolate, via a four-membered ring)"],
         ["Dimerisation, trimerisation", "benzyne + benzyne; Pd(0)-catalysed", "biphenylene; triphenylene (3 arynes, Pd)", "the last is a metal-templated [2+2+2]"],
         ["Radical/ligand", "transition-metal arynes (Ni, Zr, Pt complexes)", "stable benzyne–metal complexes", "e.g. Zr(η<sup>2</sup>-C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>)Cp<sub>2</sub>(PMe<sub>3</sub>)"],
         ["Multi-component coupling", "aryne + isocyanide + carbonyl; aryne + DMF + NaN<sub>3</sub>", "one-pot ortho difunctionalisation", "typical modern application of arynes in diversity synthesis"]],
        widths=[20, 28, 26, 26])
    B.h2("12.13  Heteroarynes and cycloalkynes")
    B.ul(["<b>3,4-Pyridynes</b> (from 3-halopyridines + base: 3-bromopyridine + NaNH<sub>2</sub> → 3- and 4-aminopyridine mixture; 4-halopyridine similarly) are the reason that meta- and para-halopyridines give mixtures with amide, but 2-halopyridines undergo normal S<sub>N</sub>Ar. 2,3-Pyridynes also form.",
          "<b>Indolynes</b> (3,4-, 4,5-, 5,6-, 6,7-indolyne) are made from bromoindoles with strong base, or from silyl triflates; Garg used them in total synthesis of indolactam V, tubingensin A, and the distortion model above explains their regioselectivity.",
          "<b>Cyclohexyne, cycloheptyne</b> exist only as intermediates (trapped by Diels–Alder, e.g. cyclohexyne from 1-chloro-cyclohexene + NaNH<sub>2</sub> (Wittig, Scardiglia) or from Kobayashi precursors); <b>cyclooctyne</b> is the smallest isolable cycloalkyne (bp 90 °C/ 65 mmHg), and strained dibenzocyclooctynes (DIBO, DBCO) enable copper-free click chemistry (Bertozzi, Nobel Prize 2022).",
          "<b>1,2-Cyclohexadiene, 1,2,3-cyclohexatriene</b> (Garg 2012): strained cyclic allenes and cumulenes generated from silyl triflates and trapped in cycloadditions; the logical extension of the aryne concept."])
    B.h2("12.14  Three pathways to nucleophilic aromatic substitution compared")
    B.table(["", "S<sub>N</sub>Ar (addition–elimination)", "Benzyne (elimination–addition)", "S<sub>N</sub>1 (diazonium)", "S<sub>RN</sub>1 (radical chain)"],
        [["Substrate", "ArX with strong EWG (NO<sub>2</sub>, CN, COR) ortho/para to X", "ArX with ortho-H and no activating group", "ArN<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>", "ArX (X = I, Br) + photostimulation; no EWG needed"],
         ["Reagent", "Nu<sup>−</sup> or neutral nucleophile", "very strong base (NH<sub>2</sub><sup>−</sup>, RLi, LDA) at low T", "H<sub>2</sub>O/Δ (phenol), HX/Cu, KI, H<sub>3</sub>PO<sub>2</sub>", "enolate, thiolate, amide; electron-transfer initiation"],
         ["Intermediate", "Meisenheimer complex (carbanion delocalised into EWG)", "benzyne then aryl anion", "aryl cation (very unstable), or radical (Cu)", "radical anion ArX<sup>•−</sup> → Ar•"],
         ["Leaving-group order", "F ≫ Cl ≈ Br &gt; I (rate-limiting addition)", "Br &gt; I &gt; Cl ≫ F (elimination)", "N<sub>2</sub>", "I &gt; Br &gt; Cl"],
         ["Regiochemistry", "ipso only; Nu enters where X was", "ipso + cine mixtures (nucleophile at either end of ‘triple bond’)", "ipso", "ipso"],
         ["Key evidence", "isolable Meisenheimer salts; second-order kinetics", "<sup>14</sup>C scrambling; trapping; no reaction when ortho-H are blocked", "N<sub>2</sub> evolution; salt-free arenes", "chain inhibition by radical traps"]],
        widths=[14, 24, 24, 18, 20])
    B.warn("<b>Typical errors in benzyne questions.</b> (1) Treating chlorobenzene + NaOH (aqueous, 300 °C Dow process) as benzyne: the Dow process includes benzyne-type cine products with some substrates, but plain chlorobenzene + NaOH at 623 K, 300 atm gives phenol (ipso/cine mixtures confirmed by <sup>14</sup>C labelling). (2) Forgetting that p-substituted halobenzenes give two products and o-substituted ones can give one or two. (3) Choosing the nitro-substituted halide: 1-chloro-2,4-dinitrobenzene reacts by S<sub>N</sub>Ar, not benzyne. (4) Drawing benzyne with a linear triple bond. (5) Assuming the NH<sub>2</sub><sup>−</sup> adds with the usual Markovnikov-like selectivity. (6) Thinking benzyne has a stable carbanion (aryl anion lone pair is in an sp<sup>2</sup> orbital, orthogonal to the π system, not delocalised). (7) Missing that the ortho-H must be present: 2-bromo-1,3-dimethylbenzene cannot form a benzyne.")


def mechanisms(B):
    c = []
    c.append(ex("12.6", "meta-Selectivity: 3-methoxybenzyne + NH<sub>2</sub><sup>−</sup> → m-anisidine (carbanion adjacent to OMe)",
        "[C:1]1#[C:2][C:3]([O:4][CH3:5])=[CH:6][CH:7]=[CH:8]1.[NH2-:20]",
        [St([A(('lp', 20), ('nb', 20, 1), 0.5), A(('b', 1, 2), ('lp', 2), -0.5)], "attack at C-1", carbanions=[2])],
        None,
        "The nucleophile adds to the benzyne carbon farther from the OMe group (C-1), so the developing aryl carbanion lies on C-2, next to the electronegative oxygen where the σ-withdrawing (−I) effect stabilises the localised in-plane lone pair. The aryl anion (an sp<sup>2</sup> lone pair in the ring plane) is then protonated by NH<sub>3</sub> (the solvent) and the product is meta-anisidine only. The same product is formed from 2-bromoanisole (benzyne with OMe at C-3) and from 3-bromoanisole (via deprotonation at C-2, between OMe and Br).", width=680))
    c.append(ex("12.7", "Dimerisation of benzyne to biphenylene ([2+2])",
        "[C:1]1#[C:2][CH:3]=[CH:4][CH:5]=[CH:6]1.[C:11]1#[C:12][CH:13]=[CH:14][CH:15]=[CH:16]1",
        [St([A(('b', 1, 2), ('nb', 1, 11), 0.5), A(('b', 11, 12), ('nb', 2, 12), -0.5)], "[2+2]", expect="c1ccc2c(c1)-c1ccccc1-2")],
        "c1ccc2c(c1)-c1ccccc1-2",
        "In the absence of a trap, the strongly electrophilic and strongly dienophilic benzyne reacts with a second molecule: the in-plane π bonds of two molecules combine to form the two C–C σ bonds of a cyclobutadiene-like four-membered ring flanked by two benzene rings (biphenylene). A third molecule gives triphenylene (the trimer, more common with Pd(0) catalysis).", width=680))
    c.append(ex("12.8", "Aryne ene reaction: benzyne + propene → allylbenzene",
        "[C:1]1#[C:2][CH:3]=[CH:4][CH:5]=[CH:6]1.[CH2:10]=[CH:11][CH2:12][H:13]",
        [St([A(('b', 10, 11), ('nb', 1, 10), 0.5), A(('b', 12, 13), ('nb', 11, 12), 0.5), A(('b', 1, 2), ('nb', 2, 13), -0.5)], "concerted ene (six-centre TS)")],
        None,
        "The benzyne acts as an enophile: the C=C π bond of propene forms a new C–C σ bond to one aryne carbon, the allylic C–H bond shifts to the other aryne carbon, and the double bond migrates; all three electron pairs move in a single six-membered transition state. The product is allylbenzene (3-phenylprop-1-ene). In competition, benzyne also adds [2+2] to alkenes, the ene reaction being preferred when allylic H are available.", width=680))
    c = [x for x in c if x is not None]
    B.grid(c, 2)


def problems(B, q):
    q(11, "Which of the following aryl halides cannot form a benzyne with NaNH<sub>2</sub>/NH<sub>3</sub>: (a) bromobenzene, (b) 2-bromo-1,3-dimethylbenzene, (c) 4-bromotoluene, (d) 1-bromo-2,4,6-trimethylbenzene, (e) 3-bromoanisole?",
      S([("a", "Brc1ccccc1"), ("b", "Cc1cccc(C)c1Br"), ("c", "Cc1ccc(Br)cc1"), ("d", "Cc1cc(C)c(Br)c(C)c1"), ("e", "COc1cccc(Br)c1")]), "(b) and (d)", "Benzyne formation requires an H on a carbon ortho to the leaving group. Both ortho positions of (b) and (d) carry CH<sub>3</sub>, so no elimination; in S<sub>N</sub>Ar there is no such requirement (but no EWG either), so these halides are essentially inert to amide.")
    q(12, "How many isomeric toluidines (aminotoluenes) are formed from (a) o-chlorotoluene, (b) m-chlorotoluene, (c) p-chlorotoluene with KNH<sub>2</sub>/NH<sub>3</sub>?",
      S([("a", "Cc1ccccc1Cl"), ("b", "Cc1cccc(Cl)c1"), ("c", "Cc1ccc(Cl)cc1")]), "(a) 2 (o- and m-); (b) 3 (o-, m-, p-); (c) 2 (m- and p-)", "(a) only one ortho H (C-6): 3-methylbenzyne, attack at C-1 (→ o-toluidine) or C-6 (→ m-toluidine). (b) two ortho Hs: C-2 gives 3-methylbenzyne (o- and m-) and C-6 gives 4-methylbenzyne (m- and p-); total three. (c) 4-methylbenzyne: attack at either carbon gives m- or p-toluidine in a ca. 1 : 1 ratio.")
    q(13, "o-Bromoanisole, m-bromoanisole and p-bromoanisole each react with KNH<sub>2</sub>/NH<sub>3</sub>. Predict the amine(s) formed in each case.",
      S([("o", "COc1ccccc1Br"), ("m", "COc1cccc(Br)c1"), ("p", "COc1ccc(Br)cc1")]), "o → m-anisidine (only); m → m-anisidine (only); p → m- and p-anisidine (≈ 1 : 1)", "OMe is −I at the carbanion position: the carbanion forms adjacent to OMe, so Nu attacks the carbon meta to OMe. For the p-isomer both aryne carbons are meta/para to OMe and the inductive differentiation is weak, giving a nearly equal mixture.")
    q(14, "Complete and balance the sequence: anthranilic acid + isoamyl nitrite (aprotic, Δ) + anthracene.",
      S([("", "Nc1ccccc1C(=O)O"), ("→", "c1ccc2c(c1)C1c3ccccc3C2c2ccccc12")]), "triptycene (+ CO<sub>2</sub> + N<sub>2</sub> + 2 isoamyl alcohol)", "The diazonium carboxylate generates benzyne, which adds across the 9,10-positions of anthracene (Diels–Alder) to give triptycene, a rigid three-bladed paddle-wheel hydrocarbon (Wittig 1956).")
    q(15, "(Assertion–Reason) Assertion: the reaction of chlorobenzene with KNH<sub>2</sub>/NH<sub>3</sub> gives a 1 : 1 mixture of aniline-1-<sup>14</sup>C and aniline-2-<sup>14</sup>C when chlorobenzene is labelled at C-1. Reason: the nucleophile adds equally to the two carbons of a symmetrical benzyne intermediate.",
      None, "both correct; the reason explains the assertion", "The symmetrical intermediate makes C-1 and C-2 equivalent, so label ends up equally at both positions. A direct displacement (ipso) would give 100 % at C-1.")
    q(16, "The reaction of 1-chloro-2-nitrobenzene with NaOH (aq) gives o-nitrophenol, while chlorobenzene + NaOH under the same conditions does not react. Identify the mechanism in each case.",
      None, "o-chloronitrobenzene: S<sub>N</sub>Ar (addition–elimination); chlorobenzene: no reaction (needs benzyne conditions, NaNH<sub>2</sub>, or 623 K/300 atm)", "The ortho NO<sub>2</sub> stabilises the Meisenheimer anion by resonance, so addition of HO<sup>−</sup> is feasible. Chlorobenzene has no activating group; at very high T the Dow process proceeds in part by benzyne (some cine substitution).")
    q(17, "Give the product of benzyne + (a) furan, (b) cyclopentadiene, (c) tetraphenylcyclopentadienone, (d) 2-pyrone. Which loses a small molecule?",
      S([("a", "C1=CC2OC1c1ccccc12"), ("b", "C1=CC2CC1c1ccccc12"), ("c", "c1ccc(cc1)-c1c(-c2ccccc2)c(-c2ccccc2)c2ccccc2c1-c1ccccc1"), ("d", "c1ccc2ccccc2c1")]), "(a) 1,4-epoxy-1,4-dihydronaphthalene; (b) benzonorbornadiene; (c) 1,2,3,4-tetraphenylnaphthalene (loses CO); (d) naphthalene (loses CO<sub>2</sub>)", "(c) and (d): the bicyclic adduct has a bridging C=O (ketone) or O–C(=O) that extrudes CO or CO<sub>2</sub> in a retro-Diels–Alder (cheletropic), aromatising the new ring.")
    q(18, "What is the product of o-bromofluorobenzene with NaNH<sub>2</sub>/NH<sub>3</sub>(l)? Explain the regiochemistry.",
      S([("", "Fc1ccccc1Br"), ("→", "Nc1cccc(F)c1")]), "m-fluoroaniline", "The only ortho hydrogen is at C-6 (next to Br); loss of H and Br<sup>−</sup> gives 3-fluorobenzyne. The nucleophile adds to C-6, the carbon remote from F, so that the aryl carbanion is on C-1 adjacent to the electronegative fluorine (−I stabilisation). Protonation gives the meta isomer.")
    q(19, "Account for the following: 2-chloropyridine + KNH<sub>2</sub> gives 2-aminopyridine (Chichibabin-like S<sub>N</sub>Ar); 3-bromopyridine + KNH<sub>2</sub> gives a 1 : 1 mixture of 3- and 4-aminopyridine.",
      S([("", "Brc1cccnc1"), ("→", "Nc1cccnc1.Nc1ccncc1")]), "3-bromopyridine forms 3,4-pyridyne; 2-halopyridine reacts by S<sub>N</sub>Ar", "In 2-halopyridine the ring nitrogen stabilises the Meisenheimer anion (like an ortho/para nitro group). 3-Halopyridine has no such activation, so the strong base eliminates HBr to 3,4-pyridyne; the amide then adds at C-3 or C-4 in nearly equal amounts (cine substitution).")
    q(20, "A student heats 2,6-dimethylbromobenzene with NaNH<sub>2</sub>/NH<sub>3</sub> and recovers it unchanged. Another student treats 2,4-dinitrobromobenzene with aqueous ammonia and obtains 2,4-dinitroaniline quickly. Explain both.",
      None, "no ortho H: no benzyne; but strongly activated ring: S<sub>N</sub>Ar", "The first lacks a hydrogen ortho to Br (both are CH<sub>3</sub>) and has no EWG; the second has activating nitro groups ortho and para to Br and reacts by addition–elimination via a Meisenheimer complex.")
    q(21, "Why does fluorobenzene give benzyne with PhLi or LDA, but the benzyne from fluorobenzene is formed more slowly with KNH<sub>2</sub> than that from bromobenzene?",
      None, "C–F bond is strong and F<sup>−</sup> is a poor leaving group, although the ortho-H is more acidic", "The first step (deprotonation) is easier for F because of its strong −I effect; the aryl-lithium or aryl-potassium is formed readily but the loss of fluoride (strong C–F bond, 125 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>) is slow, so for KNH<sub>2</sub> the elimination step is rate-limiting (E1cB) and H/D exchange is faster than substitution. Bromide is a much better leaving group; with bromobenzene proton removal is rate-limiting.")
    q(22, "Compare the reaction of m-chloro-trifluoromethylbenzene vs. o-chloro-trifluoromethylbenzene with KNH<sub>2</sub> (Roberts, 1956). Which product(s) form and why?",
      None, "o-chloro: m-CF<sub>3</sub>-aniline only; m-chloro: m-CF<sub>3</sub>-aniline predominantly", "CF<sub>3</sub> is strongly −I. In 3-CF<sub>3</sub>-benzyne the nucleophile adds to C-1, remote from CF<sub>3</sub>, giving the anion on C-2 adjacent to CF<sub>3</sub>. Both substrates therefore lead to 3-(trifluoromethyl)aniline (meta), exactly as for OMe and F.")
    q(23, "The Stobbe-type (Caubère) arylation: benzyne is generated from bromobenzene in the presence of the sodium enolate of acetone. Give the product and name the reaction type.",
      S([("", "CC(=O)Cc1ccccc1")]), "phenylacetone (1-phenylpropan-2-one)", "The enolate carbon adds to benzyne (nucleophilic addition) and the aryl anion is protonated. This is a useful transition-metal-free α-arylation of ketones; with excess base, di-arylation can occur.")
    q(24, "o-Bromofluorobenzene is treated with magnesium in THF at room temperature in the presence of furan. Name the intermediate and the final product.",
      S([("", "C1=CC2OC1c1ccccc12")]), "o-fluorophenylmagnesium bromide, then benzyne (loss of MgBrF); 1,4-epoxy-1,4-dihydronaphthalene", "Mg inserts selectively into the C–Br bond to give the ortho-fluoro Grignard reagent, which eliminates MgBrF at room temperature to give benzyne; furan traps it in a Diels–Alder reaction (Wittig, 1942).")
    q(25, "m-Bromoanisole is treated with KNH<sub>2</sub> in liquid ammonia. How many isomeric anisidines are possible by benzyne pathways, and which is actually obtained?",
      S([("", "COc1cccc(Br)c1"), ("→", "COc1cccc(N)c1")]), "3 possible (o-, m-, p-); m-anisidine is obtained", "Two ortho H (C-2 and C-6). Removal of C-2–H (between Br and OMe, the most acidic) gives 3-methoxybenzyne: attack at the carbon remote from OMe gives m-anisidine (attack at the adjacent carbon would give o-anisidine). Removal of C-6–H gives 4-methoxybenzyne with m- and p-anisidine as possible products. Experimentally the more acidic C-2 route and the −I orientation rule give essentially only m-anisidine.")
    q(26, "Why does the 1,4-didehydrobenzene (p-benzyne) formed from enediynes cleave DNA, while ortho-benzyne reacts with nucleophiles?",
      None, "p-benzyne is a σ,σ-diradical; ortho-benzyne is a strained electrophilic alkyne", "p-Benzyne has two radical centres at para positions; it abstracts two H-atoms (from the sugar C–H bonds of DNA) to give benzene, creating DNA radicals that cleave the strand. ortho-Benzyne has a closed-shell electronic structure with a polarised in-plane bond that accepts nucleophiles and cycloaddition partners.")
    q(27, "Give the products of (a) benzyne + NH<sub>3</sub>(l)/NH<sub>2</sub><sup>−</sup>, (b) benzyne + CH<sub>3</sub>OH/CH<sub>3</sub>O<sup>−</sup>, (c) benzyne + PhLi, (d) benzyne + N-methylpyrrole.",
      S([("a", "Nc1ccccc1"), ("b", "COc1ccccc1"), ("c", "c1ccc(cc1)-c1ccccc1"), ("d", "CN1C2C=CC1c1ccccc12")]), "(a) aniline; (b) anisole; (c) biphenyl; (d) N-methyl-1,4-dihydronaphthalen-1,4-imine (Diels–Alder adduct)", "Nucleophilic additions give the aryl anion which is protonated (a, b) or, in (c), gives aryl lithium and then protonation (biphenyl after work-up). The pyrrole acts as the diene.")
    q(28, "Explain why the nitro group in 1-chloro-4-nitrobenzene accelerates S<sub>N</sub>Ar but 4-chlorotoluene with the same reagents (NH<sub>2</sub><sup>−</sup>) reacts via benzyne.",
      None, "NO<sub>2</sub> stabilises the Meisenheimer anion; CH<sub>3</sub> cannot, so the strong base removes the ortho H", "In 1-chloro-4-nitrobenzene the NO<sub>2</sub> (−M, para) delocalises the negative charge of the addition intermediate. In 4-chlorotoluene there is no stabilisation, so S<sub>N</sub>Ar is impossible; the base instead deprotonates the ortho H giving 4-methylbenzyne.")
    q(29, "A mixture of 3- and 4-aminopyridine is obtained from 3-bromopyridine and KNH<sub>2</sub>, but only 4-aminopyridine from 4-bromopyridine. Explain.",
      None, "3,4-pyridyne from 3-bromopyridine gives both; 4-bromopyridine reacts partly by S<sub>N</sub>Ar", "4-Bromopyridine has N para to the leaving group: strongly activated for S<sub>N</sub>Ar (the Meisenheimer anion puts charge on N), so ipso substitution dominates; the 3-isomer lacks that activation and goes through 3,4-pyridyne, with attack at either carbon.")
    q(30, "1-Bromo-3-methoxy-2-methylbenzene is treated with NaNH<sub>2</sub>/NH<sub>3</sub>. How many benzyne intermediates and how many isomeric amines are possible?",
      None, "1 benzyne, 2 amines", "The only hydrogen ortho to Br is at C-6 (C-2 carries CH<sub>3</sub>), so there is one aryne (C-1≡C-6, with CH<sub>3</sub> at C-2 and OMe at C-3). The nucleophile can add at C-1 or C-6, giving two isomeric amines: 3-methoxy-2-methylaniline (Nu at C-1, anion at C-6) and 4-methoxy-3-methylaniline (Nu at C-6, anion at C-1 next to the electron-releasing CH<sub>3</sub>, which destabilises it). The alkyl group therefore favours the first, but the effect is small, so a mixture is expected.")
